{"skill":{"slug":"minimax-understand-image","displayName":"minimax-understand-image"},"version":{"version":"1.0.3","createdAt":1771917444094,"changelog":"minimax-understand-image 1.0.3\n\n- 流程优化：首次配置流程更为简洁，后续可直接调用图像分析功能。\n- API Key 自动获取逻辑修改：默认从 ~/.openclaw/agents/main/agent/auth-profiles.json 查找，而非此前的路径。\n- 清理文档内容，去除冗余完整流程示例，提升可读性。\n- 步骤描述更明确，帮助用户更快完成配置和调用。","changelogSource":"user","license":null,"files":[{"path":"_meta.json","size":143,"sha256":"f553b03009e5213b3eef052bfeaad6facab20ecbf47c4f2f4a281cd89d165bc6","contentType":"application/json"},{"path":"SKILL.md","size":3949,"sha256":"1f6d6c3ccf2a29e0dd360cb318bcac046f302f8615ee877d595a9285eeefeeb8","contentType":"text/markdown"},{"path":"scripts/understand_image.py","size":3746,"sha256":"1e52dd71b090d1f6f7565974c45bf9aa7d77f23f8cbf5a0b68d8e6591badfa43","contentType":"text/x-python-script"},{"path":"skill-card.md","size":2202,"sha256":"643741d2a318bbda13fb20be02ff13e149e0034b6b66d4afb5f1275ecc95197e","contentType":"text/markdown"}],"security":{"status":"suspicious","hasWarnings":true,"checkedAt":1789071157521,"model":null,"hasScanResult":true,"sha256hash":"a6a0714b0ffd78fa82b94bd6165ca408da3c9e74660632d03547ba3f32aee437","virustotalUrl":"https://www.virustotal.com/gui/file/a6a0714b0ffd78fa82b94bd6165ca408da3c9e74660632d03547ba3f32aee437","scanners":{"vt":{"status":"suspicious","verdict":"suspicious","normalizedStatus":"suspicious","analysis":"Type: OpenClaw Skill\nName: minimax-understand-image\nVersion: 1.0.3\n\nThe skill is classified as suspicious primarily due to the `SKILL.md` instructions. It directs the agent to execute `curl -LsSf https://astral.sh/uv/install.sh | sh` for dependency installation, which is a significant supply chain vulnerability as it executes an arbitrary script from the internet without prior inspection. Additionally, `SKILL.md` instructs the agent to read `~/.openclaw/agents/main/agent/auth-profiles.json` to find API keys, which, while intended for a legitimate purpose, could expose sensitive data if the agent is maliciously prompted to misuse this capability. The `understand_image.py` script itself appears to handle inputs and API keys securely via JSON-RPC, but the installation and configuration steps in `SKILL.md` introduce notable risks.","source":"palm","checkedAt":1779164827991},"skillspector":{"status":"suspicious","normalizedStatus":"suspicious","score":100,"severity":"CRITICAL","recommendation":"DO_NOT_INSTALL","issueCount":25,"checkedAt":1789071157521},"llm":{"status":"suspicious","verdict":"suspicious","normalizedStatus":"suspicious","confidence":"high","summary":"This image-analysis skill is mostly purpose-aligned, but it asks users to run unverified remote installers and launches an unpinned third-party MCP package with broad access to credentials and the user's environment.","dimensions":[{"detail":"scripts/understand_image.py starts a third-party MCP subprocess and passes the full parent environment plus the MiniMax API key, which is broader access than needed for image analysis.","label":"Credentials","name":"environment_proportionality","rating":"concern"},{"detail":"The skill recommends curl-to-shell installation for uv and installs or invokes minimax-coding-plan-mcp through uvx without a pinned version or checksum, creating mutable supply-chain execution risk.","label":"Install Mechanism","name":"install_mechanism","rating":"concern"},{"detail":"The instructions broadly trigger on image analysis requests and include setup, credential discovery, credential storage, dependency installation, and remote service invocation without clear permission boundaries or a clear privacy warning before images and prompts are sent externally.","label":"Instruction Scope","name":"instruction_scope","rating":"concern"},{"detail":"The skill stores the API key persistently in ~/.openclaw/config/minimax.json and looks for MiniMax credentials in an auth profile file. It does not show backdoor persistence or privilege escalation, but credential handling is sensitive and under-scoped.","label":"Persistence & Privilege","name":"persistence_privilege","rating":"concern"},{"detail":"The stated purpose is MiniMax image understanding, and the script does call a MiniMax MCP image-analysis tool with an image source and prompt. External API use, local image paths or URLs, and a MiniMax API key are coherent with that purpose.","label":"Purpose & Capability","name":"purpose_capability","rating":"note"}],"guidance":"Install only after reviewing the setup commands and trusting the MiniMax MCP package source. Prefer installing uv through a trusted package manager, pinning the MCP package version, avoiding mirror-based installs unless approved, and limiting what environment variables are passed to the subprocess. Treat submitted images and prompts as data sent to a third-party service, and avoid using this skill on sensitive screenshots, documents, or photos unless that is acceptable.","findings":"[T03] unexpected: Confirmed in SKILL.md: uv installation is recommended via remote script piped directly to sh. Installing uv is related to setup, but this execution pattern is unnecessarily high-risk.\n[T08] unexpected: Confirmed in SKILL.md and scripts/understand_image.py: minimax-coding-plan-mcp is invoked without a pinned version, and the child process receives the full inherited environment.\n[TM2] unexpected: Confirmed occurrences of curl ... | sh in the setup instructions. These are manual instructions rather than hidden automatic execution, so this supports Review rather than malicious.\n[E2] unexpected: Confirmed env={**os.environ, **env}. Passing MINIMAX_API_KEY is expected, but forwarding unrelated environment variables to the MCP process is not necessary for the stated purpose.\n[RP1] unexpected: Confirmed uvx minimax-coding-plan-mcp and uvx install minimax-coding-plan-mcp are unpinned. This is a supply-chain concern, not evidence of intentional abuse.\n[SDI-2] expected: The skill instructs looking for MiniMax-related keys in an OpenClaw auth profile and says to ask the user before using a match. The behavior is credential-sensitive but partially user-controlled and related to the service being used.\n[RA2] expected: Persistent storage of the MiniMax API key is explicitly documented and supports reuse, but plaintext long-lived credential storage deserves user review.\n[SQP-2] expected: Sending image sources and prompts to MiniMax is core to the skill, but the artifact lacks a clear user-facing disclosure that local images or prompt content may leave the machine.\n[AST4] expected: The subprocess call uses an explicit argument list with shell=False behavior, so command-injection concern is limited; the material issue is execution of an external unpinned tool.\n[TT2] expected: The taint flow reflects credential/environment data passed to the subprocess. It is not shell injection, but it compounds the broad environment exposure concern.","agenticRiskFindings":null,"riskSummary":null,"model":null,"checkedAt":1789071157521}}}}}